WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:07.182
What is one of the most versatile
communication tools everyone should have

00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:08.426
in their toolkit?

00:00:08.426 --> 00:00:12.160
The answer is asking questions.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.040
I think questions are the Swiss Army
knife of communication.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.672
A well timed question can accomplish
myriad communication tasks from building

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:22.130
trust and understanding

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.270
[MUSIC]
Fostering engagement and

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.986
calming your nerves through asking
good questions you can become a more

00:00:28.986 --> 00:00:31.747
connected and authentic communicator.

00:00:31.747 --> 00:00:33.276
My name is Matt Abrahams and

00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:37.990
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:42.590
Welcome to Think Fast Talk smart,
the Podcast.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:46.580
Today I am super excited to
have Deborah Shiffrin here.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:51.440
Deborah is a lecturer at Stanford
Graduate School of Business where she

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.895
teaches to MBA courses she co
created spontaneous management and

00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:59.450
creativity in the business ecosystem.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:03.782
Prior to joining Stanford, Deborah spent
a decade as a national public radio

00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:06.640
reporter, producer and director.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.600
Welcome Deborah.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.490
I love collaborating with you and
I look forward to our chat.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.480
&gt;&gt; Me too.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.030
&gt;&gt; Let's get started.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.880
&gt;&gt; Why questions?

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.990
What do they do for us?

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.506
&gt;&gt; There's a range of goals and
purposes we have for

00:01:21.506 --> 00:01:24.852
asking questions course
the big one is getting

00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:29.154
information MAT What time is it
&gt;&gt; It's a little bit before noon

00:01:29.154 --> 00:01:32.077
actually
&gt;&gt; So that's how that works I needed

00:01:32.077 --> 00:01:34.920
information I asked Matt and
I got the information I needed In.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.870
The second one is building relationships.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.810
So you want to use it
to build relationships.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.880
You ask people questions, you get to know
about them, they get to know about you.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.730
And then there's other goals
you can use in the workplace.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.230
You can ask questions to generate
ideas and foster creativity.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.409
Then of course, it's a darker goals
like interrogate, or intimidate, or

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.760
avoid responsibility.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.630
I think we'll take one, Matt, if it's
okay and break it down a little bit.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:06.380
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.670
I'd love to hear more.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.530
&gt;&gt; So one of the big ones I mentioned
was building relationships.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.720
So, if you're asking questions,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.180
yes ou're signalling to the other
person that you value them.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.530
You're taking time to
listen to their answers.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.920
You're telling them that their
answers matter and they matter.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.961
And the other one that I think is really
important is the type of question that

00:02:30.961 --> 00:02:34.020
comes with coaching or mentoring people.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.690
A lot of people are mentors,
a lot of leaders or

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.840
mentors to younger people
coming up in your organization.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.230
For me,
I use it mostly in my coaching practice.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.870
And I'll give you two
examples which I love.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.652
Imagine I'm talking to someone and they
are deciding whether to take a job offer

00:02:51.652 --> 00:02:56.330
I'll be talking and they'll be
weighing the pros and cons, right?

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:00.150
And then I'll stop them and
I'll say, what do you want?

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.060
And it's sort of a disruptive question.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.475
It breaks the back and
forth between the tactical decisions and

00:03:07.475 --> 00:03:10.090
it just takes things up to another level.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.660
A second one is say someone is
telling me they're feeling inadequate.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.710
When I hear that a couple of times,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:22.290
I will say How is feeling inadequate
serving you, and what is it costing you?

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.440
And it really makes people stop and
think about it.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.500
How is it supporting me and
how is it harming me?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.040
And it makes people either take action
to change it or change their mindset.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.578
&gt;&gt; I see, a key takeaway I had from what
you just said It's not just the question

00:03:39.578 --> 00:03:44.169
you ask but it's the willingness to
actually ask the question in the first

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:46.480
place and then listen to the answer.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.995
That's what can really help disrupt
the conversation or show that you really

00:03:50.995 --> 00:03:54.890
are concerned with the person and
want to foster that connection.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.940
Is that right?

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.514
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely, and

00:03:57.514 --> 00:04:01.907
you have to have some sort of
&gt;&gt; Trust built up so this isn't a question

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:05.822
you're not going to meet someone for the
first time say that what do you want but

00:04:05.822 --> 00:04:08.550
if you have a relationship
with someone of some sort but

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.520
there's some trust then you can
start using those questions.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:17.260
&gt;&gt; Clearly questions can help us achieve
many ends that are positive and prosocial.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.930
But you did mention that goals were not
always as friendly such as intimidation

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.090
and avoiding responsibility.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.310
Can you talk more about these?

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:26.980
&gt;&gt; Yes, I can.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.110
I'll mention interrogating.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:32.610
So this is another way to
get information I suppose.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.820
But you're doing it in
a very unpleasant way.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:40.950
You might try to trap someone into making
a mistake or doing a gotcha question.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.230
And so that's not very pleasant.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.692
People can use questions to intimidate,
and

00:04:45.692 --> 00:04:48.430
a lot of times that has to
do with power dynamics.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.517
So they may want to use a question
to increase their power dynamic or

00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:56.830
take advantage of a power
dynamic that already exists.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.340
And the third one is
avoiding responsibility.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.657
So, this brings me back to my improv
training days many years ago when

00:05:05.657 --> 00:05:11.432
I was learning and we are told as
improvisers not to ask questions on stage,

00:05:11.432 --> 00:05:14.850
and it may seem counter intuitive.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.754
But the reason is, say,
you and I are in a scene, and

00:05:17.754 --> 00:05:20.180
we're both creating it together.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:20.840
There's no script.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.820
So we're going back and
forth and building something.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.552
And if I keep asking you questions, you
have to do all the work to come up with

00:05:27.552 --> 00:05:31.690
all the ideas you don't want to do,
that you want to share the burden.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:35.390
And there's an example that I
think most people can relate to.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.080
So let's say we were going to go

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:37.995
to a movie
&gt;&gt; .Mmmh

00:05:37.995 --> 00:05:40.019
&gt;&gt; And we were trying to figure out where

00:05:40.019 --> 00:05:41.030
to go to dinner.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:42.950
Say there's like five of us.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.800
And some of them say, hey,
what do you want to go for dinner?

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.090
And the next person will say,
I don't know.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:50.195
Where do you want to go for dinner?

00:05:50.195 --> 00:05:52.790
[LAUGH] That could go to all five people.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.052
&gt;&gt; You I think you're describing
my entire teenage years.

00:05:56.052 --> 00:05:59.400
[LAUGH] I think that's what we did
every Friday and Saturday night.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.426
Believe me it's so happens to me now
I'm in San Francisco and there's so

00:06:03.426 --> 00:06:07.980
many options but I think the reason that
happens well eventually someone will step

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.336
up and suggest an actual restaurant but
I think that happens because one people

00:06:12.336 --> 00:06:16.296
might not want to take the responsibility
of choosing the restaurant But

00:06:16.296 --> 00:06:20.388
I think also people are afraid that if
they pick a restaurant and the rest of

00:06:20.388 --> 00:06:24.942
the group doesn't end up liking the food,
that they're somehow responsible for

00:06:24.942 --> 00:06:27.921
that so
isn't avoiding responsibility technique.

00:06:27.921 --> 00:06:31.123
&gt;&gt; Talk to me a little bit about
how tone plays out in question

00:06:31.123 --> 00:06:33.840
asking
&gt;&gt; Tone is really important tone can

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.140
turn a question negative or
positive, the same question.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.570
Alright man, let's do a roleplay.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:40.800
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'm ready.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.130
&gt;&gt; Ready? Okay. You're going to be the
employee and I'm going to be the manager.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:47.360
See how I did that?

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:48.590
&gt;&gt; Yes, ma'am.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:49.310
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:53.490
So that this is your line,
and I will respond two ways.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.380
You'll say the line I'll respond.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.320
You'll say the line.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:56.920
I'll respond again.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.240
Your line is I have a plan to
automate customer service.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.370
&gt;&gt; Alright.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:08.230
I have a plan to automate
customer service.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:11.060
&gt;&gt; What do you think it will
take to implement that plan?

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.350
Alright generally supportive.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:14.290
Say it again.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.259
&gt;&gt; I have a plan to
automate customer service.

00:07:18.259 --> 00:07:22.132
What do you think it'll take
to implement that plan?

00:07:22.132 --> 00:07:24.135
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's very different.

00:07:24.135 --> 00:07:28.313
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Just emphasize one word
that and attend to the entire tone and

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:30.230
context of the meeting.

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:34.210
&gt;&gt; So your point Point Deborah is
very clear tone really does matter

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:39.035
in question asking and
I would argue in most communication.

00:07:39.035 --> 00:07:43.615
having this conversation with you
has helped me realize that we can

00:07:43.615 --> 00:07:47.485
get better answers if we
ask better questions.

00:07:47.485 --> 00:07:50.420
So what goes into a good question?

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:56.700
I like to use words that are related
to values, words like impact.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.823
An example would be Matt,

00:07:58.823 --> 00:08:04.880
how does your decision Impact yourself and
the organization.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.450
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, yeah.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:10.210
&gt;&gt; Something like, Matt,
what's important or what's at stake?

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.773
And one that I love is say it's
at the end of a project and

00:08:15.773 --> 00:08:20.720
I'll say to you, Matt,
what can we create from here?

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.980
What can we create from here?

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.250
And that just assumes
that people are creative.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:30.675
And once that's assumed,
people rise to the occasion, and

00:08:30.675 --> 00:08:35.620
they free themselves to make suggestions
they might not otherwise make.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:40.384
&gt;&gt; I really like that, I like positioning
the person in the conversation in a very

00:08:40.384 --> 00:08:44.007
different perspective through
the question that you ask.

00:08:44.007 --> 00:08:45.580
&gt;&gt; Can I add one more thing?

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:46.523
&gt;&gt; You sure can.

00:08:46.523 --> 00:08:48.167
&gt;&gt; When the project is over,

00:08:48.167 --> 00:08:53.268
people often forget to ask questions that
will get a catalogue of what went well.

00:08:53.268 --> 00:08:55.720
What skills did you use to succeed?

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.473
How did your team accomplish the task?

00:08:57.473 --> 00:09:00.415
So, when it's all said and
done, you got the project,

00:09:00.415 --> 00:09:02.179
make sure you ask what went well.

00:09:04.113 --> 00:09:07.685
&gt;&gt; I love your list of questions,
it's about open questions,

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:09.093
not closed questions.

00:09:09.093 --> 00:09:13.270
It's about asking people to reflect, and
really think about the experience they

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.094
had, what it felt like, what they
learned from it, how they can apply it.

00:09:17.094 --> 00:09:20.290
Thinking about impactful words, you
like how I did that, I used one of your

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.011
impactful words as [CROSSTALK]-
&gt;&gt; Uh-huh.

00:09:22.011 --> 00:09:24.317
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And then-
&gt;&gt; Well done.

00:09:24.317 --> 00:09:27.295
&gt;&gt; And then making sure that you
ask questions about when a project

00:09:27.295 --> 00:09:27.978
is complete.

00:09:27.978 --> 00:09:33.934
That's a fantastic advice for
good questions to ask.

00:09:33.934 --> 00:09:35.520
Speaking of advice,

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.170
what advice would you give to business
leaders regarding question asking?

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:44.783
&gt;&gt; Whether you're a leader or
anywhere in the organization,

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:49.707
it takes some bravery to ask questions if
you don't know what the answer will be.

00:09:49.707 --> 00:09:50.698
As adults, it's scary.

00:09:50.698 --> 00:09:52.052
Kids ask a lot of questions.

00:09:52.052 --> 00:09:53.205
&gt;&gt; Yes, they do.

00:09:53.205 --> 00:09:54.945
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What color's the moon?

00:09:54.945 --> 00:09:56.260
And when's dinner?

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:01.070
But adults don't ask as many questions
because it's a little scary.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:04.990
You're handing the control of
the conversation to someone else.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:08.060
And you have this fear that
it could harm your psyche.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:13.526
In summary, my advice is to leave time for
questions, and be open to the answers,

00:10:13.526 --> 00:10:18.460
ones that you want to hear or
answers that you don't want to hear.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.805
And then assess how much
meeting time you spend asking,

00:10:21.805 --> 00:10:24.080
versus telling people what to do.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:25.550
So inquiry versus statements.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:30.416
&gt;&gt; That last point about speaking time,
and

00:10:30.416 --> 00:10:34.680
how they're allocated versus inquiry or
statements.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.453
We've heard that before when
Bob Sutton was on the podcast.

00:10:37.453 --> 00:10:42.667
We spent a little bit about it, and he
said from his perspective in his research,

00:10:42.667 --> 00:10:47.961
he's found that leaders who ask more
questions are perceived as better leaders,

00:10:47.961 --> 00:10:52.585
have better data to make decisions and
actually people like them more.

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:57.250
So, there's a whole lot of value to
following the advice of making sure

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.765
to leave time for questions,
be open to the answers.

00:11:00.765 --> 00:11:05.477
And then pay attention to how much
time you're actually asking questions

00:11:05.477 --> 00:11:07.610
versus just making statements.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:11.555
So thank you for
that really important advice.

00:11:11.555 --> 00:11:15.290
Now you've done a lot of question asking
throughout your life as a reporter,

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:16.930
as a coach and a teacher.

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:23.211
What are some best practices for question
asking, and does the context matter?

00:11:23.211 --> 00:11:26.181
&gt;&gt; Yes, the context definitely matters.

00:11:26.181 --> 00:11:31.420
You have to know who's in the room, what
their agendas are, what their needs are.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:35.440
So that's something you have to figure
out before you figure out the question or

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.260
even the type of question you want to ask.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.612
In terms of best practices,
I'll frame it this way,

00:11:42.612 --> 00:11:48.139
I feel like a lot of best practices
stem from a mindset of curiosity.

00:11:48.139 --> 00:11:52.061
So if I were interviewing you, Matt, but
this is not just for interviewing, but

00:11:52.061 --> 00:11:54.781
if I were an NPR reporter,
like I was interviewing you.

00:11:54.781 --> 00:11:58.901
And you say something that surprises me,
or has emotional charge,

00:11:58.901 --> 00:12:03.080
I want to follow up on that and
ask you another question.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:07.355
Sure, I've come with my list of ten
questions to ask you those questions.

00:12:07.355 --> 00:12:10.329
But I don't have to stick to that script.

00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:14.287
I can just go off script and come back.

00:12:14.287 --> 00:12:17.479
What I do as a journalist
is I have my list, but

00:12:17.479 --> 00:12:22.355
I say in advance these three questions
I absolutely need answers to.

00:12:22.355 --> 00:12:25.960
But the rest if I hear something
interesting, I go for it.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.650
&gt;&gt; That's really, really interesting.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:31.313
So if you take time to listen
thoroughly and ask questions,

00:12:31.313 --> 00:12:34.640
you can actually learn
a lot more information.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:37.103
&gt;&gt; When you think about being curious,
you're listening for

00:12:37.103 --> 00:12:39.830
information that's interesting and
surprising.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:44.524
And you're also in a state where
you're curious about the other person.

00:12:44.524 --> 00:12:48.890
And you're not just asking them questions
from your own frame of reference.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.600
Matt, if I were interviewing you-
&gt;&gt; Uh-oh.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:54.253
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I would want to do questions
that delight or engage you or

00:12:54.253 --> 00:12:56.449
questions you may not have
thought about before.

00:12:56.449 --> 00:12:59.370
So here's an example.

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:04.560
I was at my brother's house, and
he had a friend over who was a physicist.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.650
He worked at UC Berkeley as a postdoc.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:11.216
So I thought, I'm interested in physics,
but I don't know much,

00:13:11.216 --> 00:13:12.331
but I know something.

00:13:12.331 --> 00:13:14.655
I'll ask him the question that I know.

00:13:14.655 --> 00:13:20.143
So I said, are you a theoretical
physicist or an applied physicist?

00:13:20.143 --> 00:13:22.753
He answered the question,
it was okay question, but

00:13:22.753 --> 00:13:24.611
he didn't get very excited about it.

00:13:24.611 --> 00:13:27.415
So then I thought for
a minute, and I asked him,

00:13:27.415 --> 00:13:31.215
what's something surprising that
happened for you this year?

00:13:31.215 --> 00:13:34.319
And suddenly he lit up,
he got so animated.

00:13:34.319 --> 00:13:37.782
He was talking about these things
that he had done in his lab, and

00:13:37.782 --> 00:13:40.750
he was picking up things on
a table like a cup or a plate.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:45.410
And showing me how maths works
by using a cup and a plate.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:50.550
And so at the end, I found out that
he discovered something pretty cool.

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:54.670
It was connecting mathematics
to a particle that

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:58.496
scientists think is
related to dark matter.

00:13:58.496 --> 00:14:01.818
So of course, I can't understand
the full scope of what that means,

00:14:01.818 --> 00:14:04.301
not being a physicist,
but it was pretty amazing.

00:14:04.301 --> 00:14:09.163
And if I hadn't asked that question,
that never would have happened.

00:14:09.163 --> 00:14:12.587
&gt;&gt; Wow, so again, it's being curious, and

00:14:12.587 --> 00:14:17.916
really thinking about how to put
the other person in position where

00:14:17.916 --> 00:14:22.879
they can share an answer that's
really meaningful for them.

00:14:22.879 --> 00:14:26.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would say this is good for
leaders as well.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.198
When you ask someone a question,

00:14:28.198 --> 00:14:33.600
ask them a question that enables them
to speak from their perspective.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.377
So you ask those open questions,

00:14:35.377 --> 00:14:38.432
give them a chance to speak
from their base of knowledge.

00:14:38.432 --> 00:14:43.410
And not what they think you want them to
say based on your frame of knowledge.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:44.670
&gt;&gt; Excellent way to summarize that.

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:47.085
Let me ask you this.

00:14:47.085 --> 00:14:51.953
Do you have a secret weapon or
trick up your sleeve that you use

00:14:51.953 --> 00:14:55.873
to get to insightful and
surprising responses?

00:14:55.873 --> 00:14:59.067
&gt;&gt; I do,
I call it the killer last question.

00:14:59.067 --> 00:15:00.922
&gt;&gt; Ooh, tell me more.

00:15:00.922 --> 00:15:04.300
&gt;&gt; And I'll have to tell you
what it is and how you ask it.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:05.799
That's a very important part.

00:15:05.799 --> 00:15:07.020
There's several steps.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:12.120
I'm talking with someone and
we've had a conversation.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.840
We've built rapport and it's time to
end the conversation or the interview.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.127
So, I say to them,

00:15:19.127 --> 00:15:23.480
is there anything I didn't ask
you that you think is important?

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:26.570
And then here's the next step,
and this is really important.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:32.220
I had to wait in silence and
be comfortable with the silence.

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:39.403
And then nine out of ten times,
the person says, I don't think so.

00:15:39.403 --> 00:15:44.149
But then you wait again, another,
it could be like eight seconds.

00:15:44.149 --> 00:15:49.124
And then they will say the most
interesting thing that they said in

00:15:49.124 --> 00:15:50.846
the whole interview.

00:15:50.846 --> 00:15:55.217
I think this happens because you
are turning the control over to them,

00:15:55.217 --> 00:16:00.200
you're handing them the mic,
you are putting them in the driver's seat.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:04.595
And so it creates a condition where
they're more likely to share something Or

00:16:04.595 --> 00:16:08.611
at the end of this conversation,
we've been talking about a lot of things,

00:16:08.611 --> 00:16:11.961
it lets them ask a question of
themselves and get you an answer.

00:16:11.961 --> 00:16:14.969
So something they're excited about.

00:16:14.969 --> 00:16:19.400
&gt;&gt; It's interesting that you bring that
technique up of waiting because waiting is

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:21.560
something I always coach people to do.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:25.246
People don't like that awkward pause,
so they'll ask a question and

00:16:25.246 --> 00:16:29.000
if nobody responds immediately,
then they'll just move on.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:29.540
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:32.681
&gt;&gt; I had never really
thought of that pause,

00:16:32.681 --> 00:16:36.644
allowing people to really
do some self reflection.

00:16:36.644 --> 00:16:41.636
And really having that be an opportunity
for people to dig deeper,

00:16:41.636 --> 00:16:44.045
and provide more information or

00:16:44.045 --> 00:16:50.230
disclose something that's a little
more personal, so I really like that.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:52.270
&gt;&gt; Let's do an experiment for
our listeners.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:52.928
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; I'm going to

00:16:52.928 --> 00:16:55.200
ask you a question that
you've never heard, right?

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:57.282
You don't know what I'm going to say.

00:16:57.282 --> 00:16:57.876
&gt;&gt; I have no idea.

00:16:57.876 --> 00:16:59.983
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm locked in this cone of silence.

00:16:59.983 --> 00:17:04.141
&gt;&gt; And then ask it to you once and
then I'll ask it in a different way and

00:17:04.141 --> 00:17:05.931
see how the results change.

00:17:05.931 --> 00:17:07.524
&gt;&gt; All right, so
I'm going to answer it twice.

00:17:07.524 --> 00:17:08.086
Go for it.

00:17:08.086 --> 00:17:10.432
&gt;&gt; Yes, here's the first question.

00:17:10.432 --> 00:17:14.024
Today's podcasts will be really great,
right?

00:17:14.024 --> 00:17:16.263
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,
this should be a great podcast.

00:17:16.263 --> 00:17:18.213
&gt;&gt; Let me ask in a different way.

00:17:18.213 --> 00:17:25.323
What is important to you
about today's podcast?

00:17:25.323 --> 00:17:29.162
&gt;&gt; I so much hope that people
listen to your passion for

00:17:29.162 --> 00:17:34.716
asking questions to really bond and
build on relationships people have.

00:17:34.716 --> 00:17:39.392
That I hope when people are done
listening that they feel

00:17:39.392 --> 00:17:43.880
they have the tools and
the energy to go do what you do.

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:47.650
&gt;&gt; So those are the two questions so
you can see the difference.

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:51.310
The first question, the way I asked it,
I didn't get any information.

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:53.633
And then did you feel
a little bit pigeon holed?

00:17:53.633 --> 00:17:56.899
Like it wasn't really what you wanted to-
&gt;&gt; There really was only one answer.

00:17:56.899 --> 00:17:59.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What am I going to say to that?

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.297
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Totally but

00:18:00.297 --> 00:18:05.689
the second question really allowed me to
give insight into the work that I did and

00:18:05.689 --> 00:18:07.954
you did in preparation for this.

00:18:07.954 --> 00:18:11.671
And really let people know
that we really want people

00:18:11.671 --> 00:18:14.470
to take good value from what it is we do.

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:17.660
So, I totally appreciate the difference.

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:21.010
And I can see why you
are a good question asker.

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:23.180
So, thank you for that.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:27.643
Now, this whole time we've talked today
Deborah has been about questions and

00:18:27.643 --> 00:18:28.395
answering.

00:18:28.395 --> 00:18:33.098
Would you indulge me and answer the three
questions that I ask of all of our

00:18:33.098 --> 00:18:35.270
guests at the end of our podcast?

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:39.873
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, if you listen to
the answers, I'll tell you anything.

00:18:39.873 --> 00:18:41.450
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I will absolutely listen.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:42.760
Question number one.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:47.928
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:18:47.928 --> 00:18:53.106
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would that be?

00:18:53.106 --> 00:18:58.380
&gt;&gt; Be present and build on others' ideas.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:02.060
This is an improve wisdom
that's come down decades and

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:06.230
decades to me today and
to people I'm teaching.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.130
&gt;&gt; Excellent, thank you.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:13.440
Let me ask you question number two, who is
a communicator that you admire and why?

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:18.790
&gt;&gt; I'm really interested
by Kamala Harris right now.

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:23.800
She is very calm, at the same time,
she's very passionate.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.710
It's a great combination.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.890
And she owns the room.

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:31.703
A lot of politicians are very stiff but
she's not, she moves but

00:19:31.703 --> 00:19:33.370
she moves with purpose.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:36.100
So when she moves,
there's a reason and it's very clear.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:43.140
And the last thing about her is that she
has mastered the smile for all occasions.

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:46.962
She has the right smile,
whether she's debating Mike Pence,

00:19:46.962 --> 00:19:49.170
or introducing Joe Biden.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:53.060
She has the right smile and
smiling can be really tricky for women.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:59.560
&gt;&gt; Tell me more about smiling and
the challenges involved in that.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:04.858
&gt;&gt; I've seen a lot of women be given
the communication advice to smile more.

00:20:04.858 --> 00:20:10.346
And I think it's really undermining
because we know what we want to say and

00:20:10.346 --> 00:20:11.950
how we want to say it.

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:17.081
And if we're given the advice smile more,
essentially we're being

00:20:17.081 --> 00:20:22.134
told to be nicer, to seem more pleasant,
to even be more feminine.

00:20:22.134 --> 00:20:27.653
And those can be really undermining
messages and I see it all the time.

00:20:27.653 --> 00:20:33.360
So, sometimes as a woman, it's hard
to remember that you own your smile.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:38.068
You own your presentation, and
that you should do what's best for you.

00:20:38.068 --> 00:20:39.844
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, absolutely.

00:20:39.844 --> 00:20:43.320
Let me ask question number three.

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:49.629
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:54.600
&gt;&gt; I want to go back to again be present.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:57.306
And I'll tell you a little
bit more about why.

00:20:57.306 --> 00:21:02.008
It keeps you grounded,
it connects you to your audience of one or

00:21:02.008 --> 00:21:07.420
a thousand, and it keeps that inner
critic, that judge away from you.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:11.831
We all have that critic that right now,
say, I'm talking to you.

00:21:11.831 --> 00:21:14.703
I am thinking about what I
said just a minute ago, and

00:21:14.703 --> 00:21:16.410
judging it, was that stupid?

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:20.174
And then right now, I'm talking to you and
I might find a judge,

00:21:20.174 --> 00:21:24.030
I might be judging myself,
what I'm saying right now is stupid.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.450
And then move on to the next question.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:26.569
I'm not listening,

00:21:26.569 --> 00:21:30.000
because I'm thinking about the stupid
thing I said a moment ago.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.225
[LAUGH] That
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:21:31.225 --> 00:21:35.751
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Judges are really powerful,
and if we can be present,

00:21:35.751 --> 00:21:40.140
we can move that judge out of the way,
that's number one.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:46.290
&gt;&gt; Number two, I would say, rigorously
prepare, but be ready to go off script.

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:47.600
You need both of those pieces.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:51.400
You really have to prepare, I know you've
said this in the past, awesome that.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:53.671
That there's no substitute for
preparation,

00:21:53.671 --> 00:21:56.340
but you need to be ready to go off script.

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:59.670
Lead with energy and
commitment to the audience.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.966
If you do that,
you will engage your audience.

00:22:01.966 --> 00:22:06.645
They see that you want to be there,
that you're excited to be there,

00:22:06.645 --> 00:22:08.871
and that you are there for them.

00:22:08.871 --> 00:22:13.060
100%, and when you do that,
it will give you more confidence.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:15.920
And confidence will improve your
credibility in front of your audience.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:19.290
&gt;&gt; I really like your recipe a lot.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:21.504
Being present and preparing but

00:22:21.504 --> 00:22:26.597
being willing to go with what's needed
in the moment, really powerful.

00:22:26.597 --> 00:22:30.315
And then of course being audience centric,
and doing so

00:22:30.315 --> 00:22:32.380
in an energetic way will help.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.733
You know Deborah, thank you so much.

00:22:34.733 --> 00:22:37.449
As we wrap up,
I find myself asking a question.

00:22:37.449 --> 00:22:39.974
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What are my takeaways from today?

00:22:39.974 --> 00:22:42.176
And the answer is abundantly clear.

00:22:42.176 --> 00:22:45.035
Questions can really
help our communication.

00:22:45.035 --> 00:22:49.973
By taking the time to plan our questions
and actively listen to the answers,

00:22:49.973 --> 00:22:52.687
we can build camaraderie and closeness.

00:22:52.687 --> 00:22:55.492
And ultimately improve our communication.

00:22:55.492 --> 00:22:58.540
Thank you so much for
your time and for your insight.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:01.811
&gt;&gt; It's been my pleasure.

00:23:01.811 --> 00:23:06.439
&gt;&gt; Thank you for listening to
Think Fast Talk Smart the podcast,

00:23:06.439 --> 00:23:10.627
a production of
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:23:10.627 --> 00:23:13.577
To learn more, go to gsb.stanford.edu.

00:23:13.577 --> 00:23:18.500
Please download other episodes
wherever you find your podcasts.

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:24.482
